## Why This Is Asked

Explores a new angle on API testing: idempotent long-running operations and eventual consistency for per-tenant exports, a common real-world pattern.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency-Key handling across retries
- Long-running jobs with 202 acceptance and polling
- Status lifecycle: PENDING -> RUNNING -> FINAL (COMPLETED/FAILED)
- Worker downtime simulation and resilience
- Per-tenant data isolation in test data setup

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test: ensure idempotency and status polling
async function testExportIdempotent(key){
  const r1 = await fetch('/exports', {method:'POST', headers:{'Idempotency-Key': key}, body: JSON.stringify({format:'csv'})});
  const j1 = await r1.json();
  const r2 = await fetch('/exports', {method:'POST', headers:{'Idempotency-Key': key}, body: JSON.stringify({format:'csv'})});
  const j2 = await r2.json();
  console.assert(j1.job_id === j2.job_id, 'Idempotent key should return same job_id');
  let status = 'PENDING';
  const job = j1.job_id;
  while(['PENDING','RUNNING'].includes(status)){
    const s = await fetch(`/exports/${job}`); const js = await s.json(); status = js.status;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate worker downtime and verify fallback behavior?
- What metrics and logs would you collect to confirm eventual consistency across multiple clients?

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates practical API versioning and deprecation testing strategies, assessing the candidate's ability to design comprehensive test coverage for versioned APIs with concrete expectations and appropriate tooling recommendations.

## Key Concepts

- Version routing via HTTP headers
- Backward compatibility validation between API versions
- Deprecation signaling and migration path implementation
- Negative testing and edge case handling

## Code Example

```javascript
describe('GET /users/{id} versioned endpoints', () => {
  it('defaults to v1 when Version header is missing', async () => {
    const res = await request.get('/users/123')
    expect(res.body).toHaveProperty('username')
    expect(res.body).toHaveProperty('email')
    expect(res.body).not.toHaveProperty('phone')
  })

  it('returns v2 format with include_email=true', async () => {
    const res = await request
      .get('/users/123?include_email=true')
      .set('X-API-Version', 'v2')
    expect(res.body).toEqual({
      username: expect.any(String),
      email: expect.any(String),
      phone: expect.any(String),
      address: expect.any(Object)
    })
  })
})

